## Test-driven development

<!--
  Concrete TDD guidance. If the team follows red-green-refactor, name
  the discipline. If not, say what discipline you actually follow —
  aspirational TDD guidance that no one follows misleads new hires
  and agents alike.
-->

- Write the test first. The failing test is the specification.
- Prefer readable-diff matchers (see `docs/TESTING.md` §AI-legibility). Vague failure messages cost agent tokens and developer minutes.
- Keep tests deterministic. Inject a clock, seed randomness, isolate DB state — see `docs/TESTING.md`.

## Making a commit

Commits follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) — enforced by the commit-msg hook (see rubric §Part 1 D15). Activate the shared template:

```bash
git config commit.template .gitmessage
```

The template opens on `git commit` with no `-m` argument. Fill in `<type>(<scope>): <subject>`, add body if the diff needs a why-not-how explanation, and save.

